// Curated training & nutrition facts. Format: [category, title, body, source?]
export const CATEGORIES = { hyp: 'Hypertrophy', nut: 'Nutrition', rec: 'Recovery', car: 'Cardio', pro: 'Programming', bod: 'Body composition' };
export const TRAINING_FACTS = [
  // ---------------- Hypertrophy ----------------
  ['hyp', 'The stretched position drives growth', 'Training a muscle where it is longest appears to produce more growth than training where it is shortest. In a 2021 study, overhead triceps extensions (long head stretched) grew the triceps about 40% more than pushdowns over 12 weeks. Prioritise the bottom of each rep: deep, controlled, and never cut short.', 'Maeo et al., 2021'],
  ['hyp', 'Seated beats lying for leg curls', 'Seated leg curls put the hamstrings at a longer length because the hip is flexed. When researchers compared them with lying curls over 12 weeks, the seated version produced noticeably more hamstring growth. If your gym has both, the seated machine is the better default.', 'Maeo et al., 2021'],
  ['hyp', 'Lengthened partials work', 'Doing only the stretched half of a rep can grow muscle as well as, or better than, full range. Leg extensions done only in the bottom half grew the quads at least as much as full reps in one study, and calf raises in the bottom half beat full and top-half reps in another. Use them as a finisher once full reps fail.', 'Pedrosa et al., 2022; Kinoshita et al., 2023'],
  ['hyp', 'Mechanical tension is the main driver', 'The best-supported trigger for muscle growth is high tension on muscle fibres, which happens when a muscle contracts hard against load, especially near failure. Pump, burn and soreness can come along with that, but they are not the cause. Judge a set by how hard the muscle worked, not by how it felt afterwards.'],
  ['hyp', 'Light weights grow muscle too', 'A 2017 meta-analysis found that low loads (under about 60% of 1RM) and heavy loads produced similar muscle growth when sets were taken close to failure. Heavy loads still build more maximal strength. Anything from roughly 5 to 30 reps works for size; the effort is what matters.', 'Schoenfeld et al., 2017'],
  ['hyp', 'How close to failure you need to go', 'A 2024 meta-regression found muscle growth increases the closer sets are taken to failure, while strength gains are less sensitive to it. Sets that stop with 4 or more reps left are much less effective for size. A practical target is 0–3 reps in reserve, closer on isolation work.', 'Robinson et al., 2024'],
  ['hyp', 'Failure is not required, but close is', 'Meta-analyses comparing training to failure with stopping just short show similar growth when volume is matched. Going to absolute failure on every set mostly adds fatigue. Take isolation exercises to failure if you like, and stop compounds one or two reps short.', 'Refalo et al., 2023'],
  ['hyp', 'More sets, up to a point', 'A dose-response meta-analysis found 10+ weekly sets per muscle produced more growth than fewer than 5. Later work suggests the gains keep coming with more volume but with diminishing returns per extra set. For most people 10–20 hard sets per muscle per week is a productive range.', 'Schoenfeld, Ogborn & Krieger, 2017'],
  ['hyp', 'Frequency matters less than volume', 'When weekly sets are equal, training a muscle once, twice or three times a week produces similar growth. Frequency is mostly a tool for spreading volume so each set is done fresh. Your split hits everything twice a week, which is a good way to fit 10–20 sets in without junk sets.', 'Schoenfeld et al., 2019'],
  ['hyp', 'Soreness is not a growth signal', 'Delayed onset muscle soreness comes mostly from unfamiliar movements and eccentric loading, and it fades as you adapt to an exercise (the repeated bout effect). Muscle keeps growing long after an exercise stops making you sore. A session that leaves you not sore can still have been excellent.'],
  ['hyp', 'The pump does not build muscle by itself', 'Cell swelling and metabolite build-up have been proposed as growth drivers, but the evidence that they add anything beyond tension is weak. A big pump usually just means lots of blood flow and moderate-to-high reps. It feels productive, but it is not a reliable measure of a good session.'],
  ['hyp', 'Acute hormone spikes do not matter', 'The idea that heavy leg training boosts upper-body growth through testosterone and growth hormone spikes was tested directly. Training conditions that raised post-exercise hormones did not increase arm growth compared with low-hormone conditions. Growth is local to the muscle you trained.', 'West et al., 2010'],
  ['hyp', 'Rest longer between hard sets', 'Men resting 3 minutes between sets gained more muscle than men resting 1 minute in a 2016 study, largely because they could do more reps per set. Short rests make later sets weaker. Rest 2–3 minutes on compounds and at least 60–90 seconds on isolation work.', 'Schoenfeld et al., 2016'],
  ['hyp', 'Rep speed barely matters', 'A meta-analysis found reps lasting anywhere from about 0.5 to 8 seconds produced similar growth. Very slow reps (over 10 seconds) were worse, probably because they force lighter loads. Lift with control, don\'t bounce out of the bottom, and don\'t overthink tempo.', 'Schoenfeld et al., 2015'],
  ['hyp', 'Mind-muscle connection helps on isolation', 'In a 2018 study, people told to focus on squeezing their biceps grew them nearly twice as much as those focused only on moving the weight. The same effect did not show up for the quads. Focus on the target muscle during curls, raises and flyes; on heavy compounds, focus on moving the load well.', 'Schoenfeld et al., 2018'],
  ['hyp', 'Squats barely grow the rectus femoris', 'Full squats grew the glutes, adductors and vasti well but produced almost no growth in the rectus femoris or hamstrings in a 10-week study. The rectus femoris crosses the hip, so it stays short at the bottom of a squat. Leg extensions fill that gap, which is why they stay in your Legs A.', 'Kubo et al., 2019'],
  ['hyp', 'Hip thrusts versus squats for glutes', 'A 2023 study found hip thrusts and back squats produced similar glute growth, while squats also built the quads and adductors. Hip thrusts load the glutes at short lengths; squats and lunges load them at long lengths. Doing both, as your Legs B does, covers the full range.', 'Plotkin et al., 2023'],
  ['hyp', 'Why calf growth is so genetic', 'Calf size depends heavily on muscle belly length and where the tendon attaches, both of which are inherited. People with long Achilles tendons and short bellies have less muscle to grow. Hard, high-volume work with a full stretch still moves them, but expect slower progress than other muscles.'],
  ['hyp', 'Seated and standing calves are different muscles', 'The gastrocnemius crosses the knee; the soleus does not. With the knee bent in a seated calf raise, the gastrocnemius goes slack and the soleus does most of the work. Standing raises bias the gastrocnemius. Doing both, as you do, trains the whole calf.'],
  ['hyp', 'Pause at the bottom of calf raises', 'The Achilles tendon is springy, and bouncing reps let it do much of the work. A 1–2 second pause in the deepest stretch removes the bounce and forces the muscle to do the work. It feels much harder with the same weight, which is the point.'],
  ['hyp', 'Muscles grow unevenly', 'Different exercises grow different regions of the same muscle. Triceps, quads and pecs have all shown region-specific growth depending on the exercise used. That is why a muscle benefits from two or three different movements that load it at different lengths and angles.', 'Wakahara et al., 2013'],
  ['hyp', 'You do not need overhead pressing for delts', 'Side delts respond best to lateral raises, rear delts to flyes and face pulls, and front delts get plenty of work from bench and incline pressing. Studies of trained lifters show front delts are rarely undertrained when chest pressing volume is decent. Skipping overhead work costs you little.'],
  ['hyp', 'Cable laterals keep tension at the bottom', 'With a dumbbell, the bottom of a lateral raise has almost no resistance because the lever arm is short. A cable set low and slightly behind you loads the side delt in its more stretched position. Using both versions across the week, as your program does, covers the full range.'],
  ['hyp', 'Incline pressing for the upper chest', 'The clavicular (upper) head of the pec is more active at incline angles of around 30–45 degrees. Steeper inclines shift work to the front delts. Your incline barbell and incline dumbbell work covers this; keep the bench low rather than high.'],
  ['hyp', 'Training the triceps long head without overhead work', 'The triceps long head crosses the shoulder and is stretched most with the arm overhead. Without overhead extensions, skull crushers lowered behind the head and cable pushdowns from a lean-forward position give it more stretch than strict pushdowns. Dips also load the triceps at a long length.'],
  ['hyp', 'Chasing the stretch on curls', 'The biceps long head is stretched when the shoulder is extended, as in incline dumbbell curls. Preacher curls load the biceps hardest near the bottom, where it is long at the elbow. Lowering slowly into the bottom of each curl is where much of the stimulus is.'],
  ['hyp', 'Machines grow muscle as well as free weights', 'A 2023 meta-analysis found free weights and machines produced similar muscle growth. Machines often let you push closer to failure safely and keep tension on the target muscle. Choose the version you can load progressively with consistent technique.', 'Haugen et al., 2023'],
  ['hyp', 'Bigger responders and smaller responders', 'In a study of 585 people doing the same 12-week arm program, changes in muscle size ranged from slightly negative to nearly 60%. Genetics matter a lot. Comparing your rate of progress with someone else\'s tells you little; compare yourself with your own logbook.', 'Hubal et al., 2005'],
  ['hyp', 'Protein synthesis lasts about 1–2 days', 'After a hard session, muscle protein synthesis is elevated for roughly 24–48 hours in trained people, then returns towards baseline. That is one reason hitting each muscle about twice a week works well. Training a muscle once a week leaves several days with no stimulus.', 'MacDougall et al., 1995'],
  ['hyp', 'Eccentrics are powerful', 'The lowering phase of a rep produces a large share of the growth stimulus and can handle heavier loads than lifting. Eccentric training produces at least as much growth as concentric in studies. Don\'t drop the weight; control the descent for about 2 seconds.'],
  ['hyp', 'Drop sets save time, not muscle', 'A meta-analysis found drop sets produced similar growth to traditional sets with volume matched, in less time. They are useful for isolation exercises at the end of a session. They do not produce extra growth, so use them when time is short, not as a magic method.', 'Varović et al., 2021'],
  ['hyp', 'Regaining lost muscle is faster', 'Muscle comes back faster after a layoff than it took to build. Animal studies show muscle fibres keep extra nuclei after shrinking, which may explain it, though how long that lasts in humans is debated. A missed week or two is not a disaster.', 'Bruusgaard et al., 2010'],
  ['hyp', 'You can maintain muscle on a third of the volume', 'Young adults maintained their muscle gains for 32 weeks after cutting training to one-third of the previous volume, as long as intensity stayed high. Muscle is easier to keep than to build. In a hard week, keep the weights heavy and cut sets, not intensity.', 'Bickel et al., 2011'],
  ['hyp', 'A few weeks off costs little muscle', 'Measurable muscle loss usually takes around three weeks of complete inactivity, and much of the early drop in size is glycogen and water rather than tissue. Strength holds similarly. Illness or holidays are not worth panicking over.'],
  ['hyp', 'Strength can rise while size does not', 'Early strength gains are mostly neural: better recruitment, coordination and skill at the lift. Even later, a heavier 1RM can come from technique and practice rather than more muscle. If lifts go up but measurements don\'t, more hard sets near failure and a calorie surplus matter more than heavier singles.', 'Moritani & deVries, 1979'],
  ['hyp', 'Size can rise while strength stalls', 'The reverse also happens: higher-rep training can add muscle without much change in a heavy 1RM, because max strength is partly a skill. Both are normal. Track progress in the rep range you actually train in.'],
  ['hyp', 'Full range of motion beats partial for most lifts', 'Training through a full range generally builds as much or more muscle than partial reps, especially for the lower body. The exception is partials in the stretched position, which hold up well. Half reps at the top of a movement are the least productive way to train.', 'Pallarés et al., 2021'],
  ['hyp', 'Neck muscles are highly trainable', 'The neck responds to direct training like any other muscle, and studies show measurable growth from just a few sets per week. Neck curls and extensions with controlled reps are low risk when progressed gradually. Never use heavy, jerky loading on the neck.'],
  ['hyp', 'Bulgarian split squats load deep hip and knee flexion', 'Split squats put the glutes and quads of the front leg under load at long lengths, with less spinal loading than squats. Single-leg work also exposes and corrects side-to-side imbalances. They are hard because they work, which is exactly why they stay in your program.'],
  ['hyp', 'Exercise order matters more for strength than size', 'Lifts trained first in a session tend to improve more in strength, but a 2021 meta-analysis found exercise order had little effect on hypertrophy. Put the lift you most want to get stronger first. For size, total quality sets matter more than order.', 'Nunes et al., 2021'],
  ['hyp', 'Warm-up sets are not volume', 'Only sets taken reasonably close to failure count towards your weekly volume. Ramp-up sets prepare joints and grooves but provide little growth stimulus. Count your working sets, not every time you touched the bar.'],
  ['hyp', 'Both fibre types grow with any rep range', 'Type I and type II muscle fibres both grow with heavy and light loads, as long as sets are hard. The old idea of "high reps for slow-twitch, low reps for fast-twitch" has weak support for hypertrophy. Use a range of reps and train hard.'],
  ['hyp', 'Antagonist supersets save time', 'Pairing opposing muscles, like a row with a press, cuts session time without reducing the work you can do. Some research even shows performance holds up or improves slightly. Avoid pairing two exercises that compete for the same grip or stabilisers.', 'Robbins et al., 2010'],
  ['hyp', 'Use straps when grip is the weak link', 'If your grip fails before your back or hamstrings on rows, deadlifts or RDLs, the target muscle gets an easier set. Straps let the intended muscle reach failure. Train grip separately if you care about it.'],
  ['hyp', 'Rate of gain slows with training age', 'Muscle gain is fastest in the first year and slows each year after. In your second year, gaining around half the muscle of your first year is typical. That makes long, consistent phases more important, not less.'],
  ['hyp', 'Age 22 is prime time to build', 'Young men have high anabolic sensitivity to training and protein; the "anabolic resistance" seen in studies is mostly a feature of older adults. Your limiting factors now are consistency, food and sleep. That makes the next few years the most efficient time you will ever have to add muscle.'],
  ['hyp', 'Sarcoplasmic versus myofibrillar growth is unsettled', 'Some research suggests high-volume training can increase fluid and non-contractile parts of the muscle more than contractile proteins. The size and importance of this effect are still debated. For practical purposes, train hard across rep ranges and ignore claims of "functional" versus "non-functional" size.'],
  ['hyp', 'Pull-ups and pulldowns build similar lats', 'Vertical pulling builds the lats whether you move your body or the bar, as long as load progresses and the stretch at the top is controlled. Weighted pull-ups make loading the movement measurable. Let the shoulders rise fully at the top for the stretch.'],
  ['hyp', 'Rows for thickness is mostly myth', 'The idea that rows build "thickness" and pulldowns build "width" is gym folklore. Both train the lats and upper back, with differences mostly in which regions are loaded most. Use both, as your program does, and focus on full range.'],
  ['hyp', 'Deep knee bend matters for quads', 'Squatting and split squatting to deep knee flexion loads the vasti at long lengths. Several studies show deeper squats build more quad and glute muscle than shallow ones. Get comfortably below parallel on hack squats as long as your back stays in position.'],
  ['hyp', 'Romanian deadlifts are a hamstring stretch under load', 'RDLs load the hamstrings at long lengths at the hip, while leg curls train them at the knee. Both are needed because the hamstrings cross two joints. Keep the knees soft and push the hips back until you feel a strong stretch, not until the bar touches the floor.'],

  // ---------------- Programming ----------------
  ['pro', 'What RIR actually means', 'Reps in reserve (RIR) is how many more reps you could have done with good form. RIR 0 is failure, RIR 2 means two more reps were possible. The scale was formalised for lifting by Zourdos and colleagues in 2016 and is used to make effort measurable.', 'Zourdos et al., 2016'],
  ['pro', 'Most people underestimate RIR', 'A 2022 meta-analysis found lifters underestimate how many reps they have left by about one rep on average, and get less accurate far from failure. When you think you have 3 left, you often have 4 or 5. Calibrate by occasionally taking a safe machine set to true failure.', 'Halperin et al., 2022'],
  ['pro', 'How to judge RIR on the fly', 'Bar speed is the best cue: when reps slow down noticeably on their own, you are usually within 2–3 reps of failure. When a rep grinds, you are at 0–1. On safe exercises, test your estimate by continuing the set and counting how many more reps you actually get.'],
  ['pro', 'Double progression', 'Pick a rep range, such as 6–8. Use the same weight until you hit the top of the range on all sets, then add the smallest available increment and start again near the bottom. It is simple, self-regulating, and works for years.'],
  ['pro', 'Progress is reps first, weight second', 'Adding a rep at the same weight is progress, and often easier than adding weight. Your log shows last session\'s numbers so you can aim for one more rep on at least one set. Small, frequent wins compound.'],
  ['pro', 'Microloading keeps progress going', 'On isolation exercises, jumping 2.5 kg can be a 10–20% increase. Smaller increments, like 1.25 kg plates or cable pin adjustments, let progress continue instead of stalling. Use the smallest jump that the equipment allows.'],
  ['pro', 'Estimated 1RM formulas lose accuracy at high reps', 'Formulas like Epley estimate your one-rep max from a set of several reps. They are fairly accurate up to about 10 reps and get worse after that. Use them to track trends on the same lift, not to predict a real max.'],
  ['pro', 'Program hopping kills progress', 'Most program changes reset the logbook: new exercises, new weights, no way to know whether you are progressing. Adaptation to a program takes many weeks to show in size. The best program is the one you run long enough to see the trend.'],
  ['pro', 'Three weeks of data is mostly noise', 'Muscle growth over three weeks is too small to see in the mirror or on a scale. Judging a program over that span means reacting to water, lighting and mood. Give any program at least 8–12 weeks before judging it.'],
  ['pro', 'Prioritise lagging muscles first', 'Muscles trained first in a session and first in the week get your best effort. To bring up a lagging part, train it fresh, give it a few more sets, and never skip it. For you that means legs get your best energy, not what is left over.', 'Simão et al., 2012'],
  ['pro', 'Specialisation means trade-offs', 'To push a lagging muscle, add volume there and drop other muscles to maintenance volume, which is about a third of usual. You cannot add sets everywhere at once and recover. Plan specialisation blocks during a surplus, not a cut.'],
  ['pro', 'Junk volume', 'Sets stopped far from failure add fatigue without much stimulus. Ten sets at RIR 5 can be worth less than five sets at RIR 1. If sessions feel long but easy, cut sets and push the ones you keep.'],
  ['pro', 'Diminishing returns per session', 'There is some evidence that beyond roughly 6–10 hard sets for one muscle in one session, extra sets add little. Spreading volume across two sessions gets more out of the same total. Your twice-a-week split is built around this.'],
  ['pro', 'Splits are equal when volume is equal', 'Full-body, upper/lower, push-pull-legs and body-part splits produce similar growth when weekly sets per muscle match. Pick the split you can attend consistently. The split matters far less than turning up six times a week.'],
  ['pro', 'Keep exercises long enough to progress', 'Rotating exercises too often makes it impossible to track overload. Some variation helps regional growth, but swapping every week hurts. Keep your main lifts for at least 8–12 weeks before changing them.', 'Kassiano et al., 2022'],
  ['pro', 'Some variety builds a more complete muscle', 'Using several exercises for a muscle produced more even growth across its regions than one exercise in a 2014 study on the quads. Variety helps when it is planned, not random. Two or three staple movements per muscle, kept for a block, is a good balance.', 'Fonseca et al., 2014'],
  ['pro', 'Rep standards must be consistent', 'If depth, pauses or range change from week to week, your log stops meaning anything. A heavier weight with a shorter range is not progress. Keep each lift\'s standard identical and let the numbers be honest.'],
  ['pro', 'Push compounds less, isolations more', 'Taking heavy squats or deadlifts to failure creates large fatigue and technique risk for little extra stimulus. Stop those 1–2 reps short. Leg extensions, curls and raises can be taken to failure safely.'],
  ['pro', 'In a cut, keep the weights heavy', 'Load is the signal that tells your body to keep muscle. In a deficit you can drop a set here or there if recovery suffers, but keep intensity and rep quality. Holding strength through a cut is a win.'],
  ['pro', 'Expect stalls in a deficit', 'With less energy available, strength often plateaus during a cut, especially in its second half. Holding your numbers means you are keeping muscle. A plateau now is not a reason to change the program.'],
  ['pro', 'Missed sessions: shift, don\'t skip', 'If you lose a day, move the session to your rest day rather than dropping it. Skipping the same session repeatedly creates imbalances, and leg days are the most commonly skipped. Only skip if moving it would mean training the same muscles two days running.'],
  ['pro', 'Testing your max is unnecessary', 'For physique goals, you never need to test a true one-rep max. Rep PRs and estimated 1RM trends tell you the same thing with less risk and fatigue. Save maximal singles for if you ever compete.'],
  ['pro', 'Define a hard set', 'A hard set is one taken to within about 3 reps of failure with good technique. Count only these toward weekly volume. Volume recommendations in research refer to sets like these, not to every set performed.'],
  ['pro', 'Belts help on big lifts', 'A lifting belt gives your abdominal muscles something to brace against, which can increase stability and the weight you can move on squats and deadlifts. It does not weaken your core if you also train without it at lighter weights. Use it on heavy sets, not warm-ups.'],
  ['pro', 'Stable exercises let you push harder', 'On unstable exercises, balance runs out before the target muscle does. Machines and supported variations like chest-supported rows let the target muscle reach failure. That is why your program uses several of them.'],
  ['pro', 'Unilateral work evens out imbalances', 'With both limbs on one bar, the stronger side quietly does more. Single-arm and single-leg work forces each side to carry its own load. Start each unilateral set with your weaker side and match reps on the stronger side.'],
  ['pro', 'Weighted bodyweight exercises need logging', 'For pull-ups and dips, the real load is bodyweight plus added weight. If your bodyweight drops in a cut, the same added load is actually lighter. Holding added weight steady while losing bodyweight is still a strength win relative to size.'],
  ['pro', 'Calves need frequency and patience', 'Calves recover quickly and are used all day, so they tolerate frequent, hard training. Training them twice a week with full range and pauses is a sensible minimum. Progress is slow; consistency over months is the only thing that works.'],
  ['pro', 'Rest days are part of the program', 'Muscle is built during recovery, not during the session. One full rest day a week helps joints, tendons and motivation. Fill it with walking, food and sleep, not extra sessions.'],
  ['pro', 'Track sessions, not feelings', 'How a session feels is a poor guide: good days and bad days often produce similar numbers. The logbook is the truth. Beat it where you can, match it where you can\'t, and leave the rest.'],
  ['pro', 'Bar speed slows before failure', 'Rep speed drops progressively as a set nears failure. Velocity-based training uses this to estimate effort. Without a device, a clear slowdown is a reliable sign you are within a few reps of failure.'],
  ['pro', 'Warm up specifically', 'Two to four ramp-up sets of the first exercise, increasing weight and decreasing reps, prepare the exact movement. A long general warm-up adds little for lifting. Later exercises for the same muscles need only one quick feeler set.'],
  ['pro', 'Fatigue masks fitness', 'The fitness-fatigue model says each session creates both a fitness gain and a fatigue cost. Fatigue fades faster, so performance often jumps after a few easier days. A weaker week does not mean you lost progress.'],
  ['pro', 'Sessions do not need to be long', 'Most productive hypertrophy sessions take 45–75 minutes of focused work. Beyond that, effort and quality usually drop. If sessions are running long, cut rest-time scrolling before you cut sets.'],
  ['pro', 'Periodisation styles are roughly equal for size', 'Linear, undulating and block periodisation produce similar muscle growth when volume and effort are similar. Periodisation matters more for peaking strength. For physique, consistent progressive overload is the core.'],
  ['pro', 'Deloads: use when needed, not by default', 'Evidence for scheduled deloads in hypertrophy training is limited; a 2024 study found a one-week break mid-program did not improve growth. Take an easier week when joints ache, sleep suffers or performance drops across several sessions.', 'Coleman et al., 2024'],
  ['pro', 'Leg day goes early in the week', 'Leg sessions are the most fatiguing and the most commonly skipped. Putting one early in the week, when energy is high and life is quieter, raises the odds you do it. If a week falls apart, legs should be the last thing dropped.'],
  ['pro', 'Write down what you did, not what you planned', 'A log that records intended numbers instead of actual numbers ruins progression. Log reps honestly, including failed reps. The app shows last session so the honest number becomes the target to beat.'],
  ['pro', 'Small wins add up', 'Adding 1 rep a week to a set of 8 at the same weight is about a 3% gain in estimated 1RM. Over a year of consistent training, small weekly wins are what produce visible change. Chase the logbook, not the mirror.'],
  ['pro', 'Exercise selection follows joint comfort', 'If an exercise hurts a joint, swap it for one that trains the same muscle without pain. Pain changes technique and limits effort. Your no-overhead-pressing setup is an example: the muscles still get trained, just through different movements.'],
  ['pro', 'Rack pulls versus deadlifts', 'Rack pulls start from a higher position, reducing the range and lower-back demand while loading the upper back and traps heavily. They are a reasonable substitute when the full deadlift beats you up. Keep the pin height the same each time so progress is comparable.'],
  ['pro', 'Control rest periods for honest comparisons', 'If you rest 90 seconds one week and 4 minutes the next, your numbers are not comparable. Use the rest timer and keep rest roughly consistent per exercise. Then a beaten log means you actually got stronger.'],
  ['pro', 'Keep a reserve on the first set', 'Going to failure on the first set of an exercise often costs several reps on the following sets. Stopping the first set 1–2 reps short and pushing the last set harder usually gives more total work. Your log will show this as more consistent set-to-set numbers.'],
  ['pro', 'The best program is the one you finish', 'Adherence predicts results better than program design. A decent program run for a full year beats a perfect program abandoned in week three. Your job this phase is simply to complete it.'],
  ['pro', 'Training in a surplus versus a deficit', 'In a surplus, you can add sets and expect progression. In a deficit, the goal is to maintain performance with the same or slightly lower volume. Matching expectations to the phase prevents frustration and impulsive changes.'],
  ['pro', 'Pre-exhaust is not required', 'Doing an isolation exercise before a compound for the same muscle reduces how much weight you can use and has not been shown to increase growth. Put compounds first unless you are deliberately prioritising a lagging muscle.'],
  ['pro', 'Rest-pause sets', 'A rest-pause set takes a set to near failure, rests 10–20 seconds, then does a few more reps, repeating once or twice. It packs several hard sets\' worth of effort into less time. Use it on safe machine and cable exercises, not heavy barbell lifts.'],
  ['pro', 'Count weekly sets, not session length', 'A good program can be described as hard sets per muscle per week. That number, with progressive overload, explains most results. Use the Volume tab to check every muscle, especially legs and calves, is getting its share.'],
  ['pro', 'Grip width changes the exercise', 'Wider grips on bench and pulldowns increase the stretch on the pecs and lats but shorten the range for the arms. Narrower grips shift more work to the triceps or biceps. Pick a grip and keep it consistent so your log stays comparable.'],
  ['pro', 'Bracing is a skill', 'Taking a deep breath into the belly and tightening the trunk before each heavy rep stabilises the spine and lets you use more weight. Exhale after the hardest part of the rep or at the top. Practise it on warm-up sets so it is automatic on working sets.'],
  // ---------------- Nutrition ----------------
  ['nut', 'How much protein you actually need', 'A 2018 meta-analysis of 49 studies found the benefit of protein for muscle gain plateaued at about 1.6 g per kg of bodyweight per day, with an upper confidence limit near 2.2 g/kg. At 74 kg that is roughly 120–165 g. Your 200 g target is deliberately higher because you are in a deficit.', 'Morton et al., 2018'],
  ['nut', 'Why protein goes up in a cut', 'In a calorie deficit, the body is more willing to break down muscle for energy, and higher protein protects against that. A review on lean athletes in a deficit recommended 2.3–3.1 g per kg of lean mass. That is why your cut target is 200 g, not 150 g.', 'Helms et al., 2014'],
  ['nut', 'Four or five protein feedings beat two', 'In a 2013 study, 80 g of protein split into four 20 g doses every three hours stimulated more muscle protein synthesis over 12 hours than eight 10 g doses or two 40 g doses. Spreading protein over the day keeps synthesis elevated more often. Aim for 4–5 meals of 40–50 g.', 'Areta et al., 2013'],
  ['nut', 'Even protein distribution across the day', 'Eating protein evenly across breakfast, lunch and dinner produced about 25% higher 24-hour muscle protein synthesis than eating most of it at dinner. Most people eat a low-protein breakfast and a huge dinner. Front-loading protein is an easy win.', 'Mamerow et al., 2014'],
  ['nut', 'The 30 g per meal limit is a myth', 'A 2023 study found 100 g of protein in one meal produced a larger and much longer anabolic response than 25 g. Your body does not waste protein above 30 g. Spreading protein is still a good idea, but a big protein meal is not wasted.', 'Trommelen et al., 2023'],
  ['nut', 'The anabolic window is wide', 'A meta-analysis found that the apparent benefit of eating protein right after training disappeared once total daily protein was matched. There is no 30-minute window. Eat a protein meal within a few hours either side of training and hit the daily total.', 'Schoenfeld, Aragon & Krieger, 2013'],
  ['nut', 'Protein before bed works', 'Taking 40 g of casein before sleep increased overnight muscle protein synthesis in young men, and a 12-week study found greater gains in muscle size and strength with a pre-sleep protein supplement. Greek yoghurt, cottage cheese or a casein shake are good options.', 'Res et al., 2012; Snijders et al., 2015'],
  ['nut', 'Leucine and the per-meal dose', 'Leucine is the amino acid that most strongly switches on muscle protein synthesis, and about 2–3 g per meal seems to be enough. That comes from roughly 25–40 g of protein from meat, dairy or eggs. Plant proteins contain less leucine, so you need a bit more of them.'],
  ['nut', 'Plant protein can build muscle', 'A 2021 study found young men on a vegan diet built as much muscle as omnivores when both ate about 1.6 g/kg of protein, mostly from soy. Total protein and leucine per meal matter more than the source. Mixing plant sources covers amino acid gaps.', 'Hevia-Larraín et al., 2021'],
  ['nut', 'Protein burns calories to digest', 'Your body uses about 20–30% of protein\'s calories just to digest and process it, compared with about 5–10% for carbs and 0–3% for fat. That is the thermic effect of food. High-protein diets give you a small calorie advantage on top of better muscle retention.'],
  ['nut', 'Protein is the most filling macro', 'Gram for gram, protein reduces hunger more than carbs or fat in most studies. In a cut, that makes it your best tool against hunger as well as for muscle. Start meals with the protein portion.'],
  ['nut', 'Creatine: what it does', 'Creatine raises phosphocreatine stores in muscle by roughly 20–40%, letting you squeeze out an extra rep or two on hard sets. Over months, that extra work leads to more strength and muscle. It is the most researched sports supplement, and 3–5 g a day is the standard dose.', 'Kreider et al., 2017 (ISSN)'],
  ['nut', 'Creatine: what it doesn\'t do', 'Creatine does not build muscle by itself, burn fat, or cause dehydration and cramps, which controlled studies have repeatedly failed to show. It does add 1–2 kg of water, mostly inside the muscle, in the first weeks. Expect that on the scale and ignore it.'],
  ['nut', 'Creatine and hair loss', 'The hair loss worry comes from a single 2009 study in rugby players that found a rise in DHT, which has never been replicated, and no study has measured actual hair loss from creatine. If you are worried, the evidence does not support avoiding it.', 'van der Merwe et al., 2009'],
  ['nut', 'Creatine does not need loading', 'Loading at 20 g a day saturates muscle in about a week; 3–5 g a day gets there in about 3–4 weeks. Long-term results are the same. Take it daily at any time, with or without food, and don\'t cycle it.'],
  ['nut', 'Caffeine improves performance', 'Caffeine at about 3–6 mg per kg bodyweight, taken 30–60 minutes before training, reliably improves strength, power and endurance. At 74 kg that is roughly 220–450 mg. Higher doses add side effects, not performance.', 'ISSN position stand, 2021'],
  ['nut', 'Alcohol blunts muscle repair', 'In a 2014 study, drinking a large dose of alcohol after training reduced muscle protein synthesis by 24% even when protein was eaten, and by 37% without protein. It also adds empty calories and worsens sleep. In a cut, it costs you on every front.', 'Parr et al., 2014'],
  ['nut', 'Low-fat diets lower testosterone', 'A 2021 meta-analysis found low-fat diets reduced testosterone by about 10–15% in men compared with higher-fat diets. That is one reason your fat target sits at 75 g, not 40 g. Take calorie cuts from carbs first.', 'Whittaker & Wu, 2021'],
  ['nut', 'Carbs fuel training volume', 'Muscle glycogen fuels repeated hard sets. Very low-carb diets often reduce the number of reps you can do in high-volume sessions. Keeping carbs around your training sessions helps you hold performance during a cut.'],
  ['nut', 'Fat loss is about calories, not carbs', 'In a metabolic ward study, cutting fat produced slightly more body fat loss than cutting carbs by the same number of calories, despite lower insulin on the low-carb diet. Over a year, the DIETFITS trial found low-fat and low-carb diets produced similar weight loss. Choose the split you can stick to.', 'Hall et al., 2015; Gardner et al., 2018'],
  ['nut', 'Meal frequency doesn\'t change fat loss', 'Eating three or six times a day produces the same fat loss when calories and protein match. "Stoking the metabolism" with frequent meals is a myth. Choose a meal pattern that lets you hit protein in 4–5 feedings and controls your hunger.', 'Schoenfeld et al., 2015'],
  ['nut', 'Intermittent fasting is just a calorie tool', 'Time-restricted eating produces similar fat loss to normal calorie restriction when calories are matched. Its only advantage is that some people find it easier to eat less. For muscle, it makes the 4–5 protein feedings harder to fit in.'],
  ['nut', 'Late eating is not fattening by itself', 'Calories at 9pm are not stored differently from calories at 9am in any meaningful way for fat loss. The problem with late eating is that it is usually extra eating: snacks after a long, underfed day. Front-loading food makes the whole day easier to control.'],
  ['nut', 'People underreport what they eat', 'In a classic 1992 study, people who said they could not lose weight on low calories were underreporting intake by 47% and overreporting activity by 51%. Nobody was lying; estimation is just bad. Weigh food, especially oils, sauces and snacks.', 'Lichtman et al., 1992'],
  ['nut', 'Cooking oil is the hidden calorie', 'A tablespoon of olive oil is about 120 kcal, and a free pour can easily be two or three. That alone can erase a third of a planned deficit. Measure oil with a spoon or use spray.'],
  ['nut', 'Raw versus cooked weights', 'Rice roughly triples in weight when cooked; chicken loses about 25% of its weight as water. Logging cooked food with raw values (or the reverse) can be off by hundreds of calories. Pick one method, ideally raw weights, and use matching entries.'],
  ['nut', 'Food labels have tolerances', 'EU guidance allows nutrition labels a margin of around ±20% for many nutrients. Individual items can be off, but errors tend to average out over many foods. Consistency in logging matters more than perfect accuracy.'],
  ['nut', 'One kg of fat is roughly 7,700 kcal', 'Body fat stores about 7,700 kcal per kg, which is where the 0.3 kg a week target comes from: a deficit of roughly 330 kcal a day. It is an approximation because the body adapts and water shifts. The weekly trend tells you whether the estimate is right for you.'],
  ['nut', 'Fibre keeps you full', 'Fibre slows digestion and adds bulk with few calories, which helps control hunger in a cut. Most guidelines recommend around 25–30 g per day; most people eat less. Fruit, veg, oats, beans and wholegrain bread are the easiest sources.'],
  ['nut', 'Vegetables are the cut\'s secret weapon', 'Most vegetables contain 20–40 kcal per 100 g. A large plate of veg adds volume, fibre and micronutrients for very few calories. When hunger is the problem, more veg is the cheapest fix.'],
  ['nut', 'Liquid calories don\'t fill you up', 'Calories from drinks are less satiating than the same calories from food, so people don\'t eat less later to compensate. Juice, fizzy drinks, lattes and alcohol add up fast. In a cut, drink water, black coffee, tea or zero-calorie drinks.'],
  ['nut', 'Sweeteners are not a problem', 'Randomised trials generally show that replacing sugary drinks with artificially sweetened ones helps weight control, or at worst is neutral. Approved sweeteners are safe at normal intakes. A diet drink is a reasonable tool for a cut.'],
  ['nut', 'Fruit sugar is fine', 'The sugar in whole fruit comes with fibre, water and micronutrients and is hard to overeat. Fruit consumption is consistently linked with better health, not worse. Two or three pieces a day fits comfortably in your targets.'],
  ['nut', 'Vitamin D in Ireland', 'At Dublin\'s latitude (53°N), the sun is too low between roughly October and March for your skin to make useful vitamin D. UK and Irish public health guidance recommends a daily vitamin D supplement over winter. It is cheap and worth taking.'],
  ['nut', 'Oily fish and omega-3', 'Salmon, mackerel and sardines provide EPA and DHA omega-3 fats, which most people eat too little of. One or two portions a week is a common recommendation. Fish oil supplements are a backup if you don\'t eat fish.'],
  ['nut', 'Dairy is a high-quality protein', 'Milk, yoghurt and cheese contain both whey (fast) and casein (slow) protein, with plenty of leucine. Studies show milk after training supports muscle gain as well as many supplements. Skyr and Greek yoghurt are among the best protein-per-calorie foods.'],
  ['nut', 'Eggs and cholesterol', 'For most people, dietary cholesterol from eggs has a modest effect on blood cholesterol. Eggs are a cheap, high-quality protein. Combine whole eggs with egg whites if you want more protein for fewer calories.'],
  ['nut', 'Protein powder is just food', 'Whey protein is milk protein dried into powder. It has no special muscle-building effect beyond its protein content. It is useful because it is cheap, quick and low in calories per gram of protein.'],
  ['nut', 'Salt changes water, not fat', 'A salty meal makes you retain water for a day or two, which can add 0.5–1 kg on the scale. It is not fat. This is one of the main reasons daily weigh-ins are noise.'],
  ['nut', 'Hydration affects performance', 'Losing about 2% of bodyweight in fluid can noticeably reduce strength and endurance performance. Pale yellow urine is a simple guide. Drink regularly through the day, not just at the gym.'],
  ['nut', 'Fasted training is optional', 'For lifting, training fasted can reduce performance for some people, especially in long sessions. There is no fat loss advantage when daily calories are matched. If you train in the morning, a small protein and carb snack is a good default.'],
  ['nut', 'Diet breaks: mixed evidence', 'In obese men, alternating two weeks of dieting with two weeks at maintenance produced more fat loss than continuous dieting. In lean, resistance-trained people, diet breaks did not improve fat loss but did reduce hunger. Your 8-week cut is short enough not to need one.', 'Byrne et al., 2018; Peos et al., 2021'],
  ['nut', 'Refeeds are mostly psychological', 'A one-day high-carb refeed temporarily raises leptin and refills glycogen, but the hormonal effect fades within days. Refeeds can help adherence and training quality, but they do not "reset" your metabolism. They also count toward weekly calories.'],
  ['nut', 'Breakfast is optional but useful for you', 'Skipping breakfast does not slow your metabolism. But with a 200 g protein target, missing a feeding makes the remaining meals huge. A 40–50 g protein breakfast is the easiest way to make your target achievable.'],
  ['nut', 'Milk after training', 'Studies in young men found that drinking milk after resistance training led to greater muscle gains than soy or carbohydrate drinks with matched calories. Milk is cheap, contains both fast and slow proteins, and is easy to log. It is a solid post-workout option.'],
  ['nut', 'Nitrates and beetroot', 'Beetroot juice is rich in nitrate, which the body converts to nitric oxide. It improves endurance performance in some studies, mostly in less-trained people. The evidence for lifting is weaker; treat it as optional.'],
  ['nut', 'Beta-alanine buffers acid', 'Beta-alanine raises muscle carnosine, which buffers acid during efforts lasting roughly 1–4 minutes. It can help high-rep sets and sprint intervals slightly. The tingling it causes is harmless.'],
  ['nut', 'Supplements that mostly don\'t work', 'BCAAs add nothing if your protein intake is adequate. Fat burners, testosterone boosters and most "anabolic" herbs have weak or no evidence. Creatine, caffeine, protein powder and vitamin D cover almost all of the useful ground.'],
  ['nut', 'Carbs around training', 'Eating carbs in the meals before and after training helps fuel high-volume sessions and refill glycogen. In a cut, shifting more of your carbs around training is a sensible way to protect performance. Total daily intake still matters most.'],
  ['nut', 'Lean bulk: small surplus', 'For someone a year or two into training, a small surplus of roughly 200–300 kcal above maintenance limits fat gain while supporting muscle growth. Gaining faster mostly adds fat. Reviews suggest aiming for about 0.25–0.5% of bodyweight gained per month for intermediates.', 'Iraki et al., 2019'],
  ['nut', 'Potassium and magnesium', 'Potassium and magnesium support muscle and nerve function and are often low in diets light on fruit, veg, beans and nuts. Bananas, potatoes, spinach, beans and dairy cover them well. Magnesium supplements only help if you are actually deficient.'],
  ['nut', 'Hunger is predictable', 'Hunger usually rises as a cut goes on, especially in the last weeks. High-protein meals, high-volume foods, enough sleep and caffeine all help. Expect it rather than treating it as a sign something is wrong.'],
  ['nut', 'Weekend calories count', 'Two loose weekend days at +800 kcal each erase over two-thirds of a week\'s planned deficit. Many people diet perfectly on weekdays and lose nothing. The weekly average is what moves the scale.'],
  ['nut', 'Consistency beats precision in logging', 'Logging every day, even imperfectly, is more useful than perfect logging three days a week. Patterns show up in the averages. A slightly wrong log you keep is better than a perfect log you abandon.'],

  // ---------------- Body composition ----------------
  ['bod', 'Why weight jumps 1 kg overnight', 'Daily weight swings of 0.5–1.5 kg are normal. Each gram of stored glycogen holds about 3 g of water, a salty meal adds water for a day or two, and the food in your gut can weigh over a kilo. None of this is fat, which is why you weigh in only once a week.'],
  ['bod', 'Weekly weigh-ins beat daily noise', 'At a target of 0.3 kg per week, real fat loss is smaller than a normal day-to-day swing. One weigh-in under the same conditions each week cuts most of the noise. The trend across 3–4 weeks is what calorie decisions should be based on.'],
  ['bod', 'Fat leaves as carbon dioxide', 'When you lose fat, about 84% of its mass leaves your body as exhaled carbon dioxide and about 16% as water. Your lungs are the main exit route. Fat is not "burned off" as heat or sweated out.', 'Meerman & Brown, 2014'],
  ['bod', 'You can\'t spot-reduce fat', 'Training a body part does not burn the fat over it. In one study, six weeks of ab exercises did not reduce abdominal fat at all. Where fat comes off is decided by genetics; overall energy balance decides how much.', 'Vispute et al., 2011'],
  ['bod', 'Fat cells shrink, they don\'t disappear', 'In adults the number of fat cells stays fairly stable; about 10% are replaced each year. Losing fat shrinks the cells. That is one reason regained fat comes back to the same places.', 'Spalding et al., 2008'],
  ['bod', 'Muscle takes less space than fat', 'A kilo of muscle weighs the same as a kilo of fat, but muscle is denser, taking up roughly 15–20% less volume. The same bodyweight looks very different at different body fat levels. That is why the scale alone tells half the story.'],
  ['bod', 'The first week of a cut is mostly water', 'When calories and carbs drop, glycogen stores fall and release water, so the first week often shows a 1–2 kg drop. It is not all fat and the rate will slow. That is why calorie adjustments wait for at least three weigh-ins.'],
  ['bod', 'After a cut, weight jumps back 1–2 kg', 'When you return to maintenance, glycogen and water refill and the scale can rise 1–2 kg within days. That is not fat regain. Expect it, so you don\'t panic and cut again.'],
  ['bod', 'Body fat tools have big error bars', 'Bioelectrical impedance scales can be off by 3–5 percentage points and swing with hydration. DEXA is better but still has around 1–2% error. Use one method under the same conditions and watch the trend, not a single reading.'],
  ['bod', 'What 9–10% body fat looks like', 'For most men, abs start to show clearly somewhere around 10% body fat, depending on genetics and how much ab muscle you have. Essential fat for men is about 3–5%. At 72 kg and 10%, you would carry roughly 7 kg of fat.'],
  ['bod', 'Recomposition slows with experience', 'Losing fat and gaining muscle at the same time happens most easily in beginners, people returning after a layoff, and people with more body fat. With a year of serious training, it still happens but slowly. A dedicated cut followed by a lean bulk is usually more efficient.', 'Barakat et al., 2020'],
  ['bod', 'The natural ceiling for muscle', 'A 1995 study of lifters found that drug-free men rarely exceeded a fat-free mass index (FFMI) of about 25. At 183 cm, 72 kg and 10% fat, your FFMI is about 19. There is a lot of room to grow, but it takes years, not months.', 'Kouri et al., 1995'],
  ['bod', 'Slower cuts keep more muscle', 'In a study of athletes, losing about 0.7% of bodyweight a week led to lean mass gains, while losing about 1.4% a week did not. Slower loss gives training a better chance to protect and build muscle. Your 0.3 kg a week target sits safely in the slow zone.', 'Garthe et al., 2011'],
  ['bod', '"Starvation mode" doesn\'t stop fat loss', 'In the 1940s Minnesota Starvation Experiment, men on about 1,570 kcal a day lost roughly a quarter of their bodyweight. Metabolism slows in a deficit, but never enough to stop weight loss entirely. If you are not losing weight, you are not in a deficit.', 'Keys et al., 1950'],
  ['bod', 'Metabolic adaptation is real but modest', 'After weight loss, energy expenditure falls a bit more than the lost mass alone predicts, mostly through less spontaneous movement and more efficient muscles. It is usually a few hundred kcal at most. Watching your steps covers much of it.'],
  ['bod', 'Checking your body makes it worse', 'Experiments on body checking found that repeatedly scrutinising your body in the mirror increases body dissatisfaction, even in people without eating disorders. Frequent checking makes small flaws look bigger and progress look smaller. That is why photos are every four weeks and the home screen shows habits, not body metrics.', 'Shafran et al., 2007'],
  ['bod', 'Photos lie in the short term', 'Lighting, angle, time of day, pump and water can change how you look more than a month of fat loss. Comparison photos only mean something when conditions match. Same place, same light, same time, relaxed, every four weeks.'],
  ['bod', 'Waist measurement tracks fat loss well', 'A tape measure around the waist at the navel, in the morning, is a cheap and decent fat loss signal. Waist size falls as abdominal fat drops, while bodyweight can be masked by water. Check it with your Saturday weigh-in, not daily.'],
  ['bod', 'Extreme leanness costs hormones', 'In a case study of a natural bodybuilder preparing for a contest, testosterone fell to about a quarter of normal at stage-lean body fat, then recovered after the contest. Very low body fat is a temporary state, not a place to live. 9–10% is lean but sustainable for a while.', 'Rossow et al., 2013'],
  ['bod', 'Where fat comes off last', 'In most men, the lower belly and lower back are the last places to lean out. That is genetic and normal. When you get frustrated that your lower abs aren\'t showing, it usually means you are close, not that the plan has failed.'],
  ['bod', 'Abs come from low fat and a trained core', 'Visible abs require low body fat; crunches alone do not reveal them. But training the abs makes the muscles thicker, so they show through at slightly higher body fat. Both matter.'],
  ['bod', 'BMI fails for muscular people', 'Body mass index uses only height and weight, so muscular people often score as overweight. It is fine for population statistics but poor for individuals who train. Waist measurements and photos are better tools for you.'],
  ['bod', 'Your resting metabolism is most of your burn', 'Your basal metabolic rate, the energy to keep you alive at rest, makes up about 60–70% of daily energy expenditure. Digesting food adds around 10%. Exercise and daily movement make up the rest, which is why steps matter more than many people think.'],
  ['bod', 'Non-exercise movement is a big lever', 'In a 1999 overfeeding study, the differences in fat gain between people were largely explained by how much their spontaneous movement increased, by up to about 700 kcal a day. Walking, standing and fidgeting add up. Desk jobs push this number down.', 'Levine et al., 1999'],
  ['bod', 'Your weight is a moving average', 'Treat each weigh-in as one noisy sample of an underlying trend. A trend line through several weeks of data is far more reliable than any single point. That is what the Progress tab draws.'],
  ['bod', 'Water retention can hide fat loss', 'Stress, poor sleep and hard training can increase water retention, masking fat loss on the scale for a week or two. When the water drops, the scale catches up suddenly. This is one more reason not to react to a single bad weigh-in.'],
  ['bod', 'Glycogen and water stores', 'Your muscles store around 400–500 g of glycogen and your liver about 100 g. Each gram of glycogen holds about 3 g of water. Filling or emptying these stores can move the scale by 1–2 kg in either direction.'],
  ['bod', 'Realistic muscle gain', 'For someone in their second year of serious training, gaining around 0.25–0.5 kg of lean mass per month in a well-run lean bulk is realistic. Going from 72 to 80 kg will take many months, and part of that weight will be fat. That is normal.'],
  ['bod', 'Lean bulks gain some fat', 'Even a careful lean bulk adds some fat along with muscle; the goal is a good ratio, not zero fat gain. Expect your waist to grow slightly. Judge the bulk by strength progress and the rate of gain, not by losing your abs.'],
  ['bod', 'Waist-to-height ratio', 'A waist under half your height is a simple marker of healthy body fat distribution. At 183 cm, that means under about 91.5 cm. It is a better health marker than BMI for active people.'],
  ['bod', 'Body fat distribution is genetic', 'Where you store fat and where you lose it first is largely inherited. Two people at the same body fat can look very different. Compare yourself to your own past photos only.'],
  ['bod', 'Visceral fat responds well to exercise', 'Fat stored around the organs drops relatively quickly with exercise and calorie control. It is the type of fat most linked to health risks. Even when the mirror shows little change, your internal health may be improving.'],
  ['bod', 'Calories in and out both change', 'Energy balance always holds, but both sides respond to each other: eating less can reduce movement, and exercising more can increase hunger. That is why weekly weight trends, not calculators, decide your adjustments.'],
  ['bod', 'Brown fat won\'t solve fat loss', 'Brown fat burns energy to make heat, but adults have small amounts and its contribution to daily calorie burn is minor. Cold exposure can activate it a little. It is not a meaningful fat loss tool.'],
  ['bod', 'Scales vary', 'Different scales can read differently by 0.5 kg or more, and the same scale can vary depending on the floor surface. Always use the same scale in the same spot. Changing scales mid-phase corrupts the trend.'],
  ['bod', 'Hunger peaks near the end of a cut', 'As body fat falls, leptin drops and hunger rises. The last two weeks of a cut are usually the hardest. Knowing that in advance makes it easier to hold steady instead of switching plans.'],
  ['bod', 'Post-cut maintenance comes first', 'After a cut, moving to maintenance calories for a short period lets the body stabilise before a bulk. Weight will rise slightly from glycogen and water. Then increase calories gradually into a small surplus.'],
  ['bod', 'Muscle protects your metabolism', 'Muscle tissue burns more energy at rest than fat, though less than many claim, about 13 kcal per kg per day versus about 4.5 kcal for fat. More muscle helps slightly, but the main benefit is how you look and perform.'],
  ['bod', 'Daily weight is mostly not fat', 'On any day your weight reflects water, glycogen, sodium, gut contents and hydration far more than fat. Fat changes slowly and steadily. That is why one weigh-in a week is enough.'],
  ['bod', 'The scale and the mirror disagree', 'You can lose fat while the scale stays flat for a week due to water. You can also look flatter while losing fat due to lower glycogen. Neither short-term signal is reliable; four-week photos and weekly trends are.'],
  ['bod', 'Strength in a cut is a muscle proxy', 'If your strength holds through a cut, you are very likely keeping your muscle. Big strength drops suggest the deficit is too aggressive or protein and sleep are lacking. Use your logbook as a body composition tool.'],
  ['bod', 'Your body adapts to lower intake', 'As you lose weight, a smaller body needs fewer calories. At 72 kg you need slightly less than at 74 kg. That is why the app\'s rate-based adjustments matter more than a fixed calorie number.'],
  ['bod', 'Comparison is noise', 'Other people\'s physiques reflect their genetics, training age, lighting and editing. Comparing yourself to them gives no useful information. Your past self is the only valid comparison.'],
  ['bod', 'Body fat set point is weak', 'The idea that your body defends a fixed body fat level has limited support. It does resist rapid change, especially after big losses. Slow, steady changes are easier to sustain.'],
  ['bod', 'A good cut is boring', 'The best cuts are uneventful: same meals, same training, slow steady weight loss. Drama usually means the deficit is too big or the plan keeps changing. Boring is the goal.'],
  ['bod', 'Stress affects fat storage', 'Chronic stress raises cortisol, which can increase appetite and water retention. It does not override energy balance, but it makes the plan harder to follow. Sleep and routine are the best stress tools you control.'],
  ['bod', 'Sleep affects body composition', 'Short sleep shifts weight loss away from fat and towards muscle and increases hunger. Seven to nine hours is a body composition tool, not a luxury. It may be the most underrated part of your cut.'],
  ['bod', 'What a 0.3 kg week looks like', 'At 0.3 kg a week, you lose about 2.4 kg over 8 weeks. In the mirror, any single week looks identical. Only the four-week photo comparison will show change.'],
  ['bod', 'Keep perspective', 'A year of consistent training and sensible eating changes a physique far more than any single phase. Plans that are completed build on each other. Plans that are abandoned start from zero.'],
  // ---------------- Recovery ----------------
  ['rec', 'Short sleep turns fat loss into muscle loss', 'Dieters sleeping 5.5 hours lost the same weight as those sleeping 8.5 hours, but 55% less of it was fat and 60% more was lean mass. Same calories, worse result. In a cut, sleep is one of the main things protecting your muscle.', 'Nedeltcheva et al., 2010'],
  ['rec', 'One week of short sleep lowers testosterone', 'Young men restricted to 5 hours of sleep for a week had daytime testosterone levels 10–15% lower. That is roughly the drop expected from 10–15 years of ageing. Protecting sleep protects your hormones.', 'Leproult & Van Cauter, 2011'],
  ['rec', 'Sleep loss raises hunger', 'After two nights of 4-hour sleep, men had 18% lower leptin, 28% higher ghrelin and about 24% more hunger, especially for carb-dense foods. Short sleep makes a calorie deficit much harder to hold. Bad nights are when cravings win.', 'Spiegel et al., 2004'],
  ['rec', 'More sleep improves performance', 'Stanford basketball players who extended their sleep to around 10 hours in bed improved sprint times and shooting accuracy by about 9%. Most athletes are under-slept. Try adding 30–60 minutes and watch your logbook.', 'Mah et al., 2011'],
  ['rec', 'Caffeine has a long tail', 'Caffeine has a half-life of about 5 hours, and 400 mg taken 6 hours before bed cut total sleep by more than an hour in a 2013 study. People often don\'t notice the effect. Keep caffeine before about 2pm if you sleep around 11pm.', 'Drake et al., 2013'],
  ['rec', 'Alcohol wrecks sleep quality', 'Alcohol helps you fall asleep faster but suppresses REM sleep and fragments the second half of the night. You wake less rested even after a normal number of hours. Even moderate drinking measurably worsens sleep.'],
  ['rec', 'Ice baths can blunt muscle growth', 'Over 12 weeks, people who used cold water immersion after training gained less muscle and strength than those who did active recovery. Cold reduces the inflammatory signalling that drives adaptation. Avoid ice baths right after lifting.', 'Roberts et al., 2015'],
  ['rec', 'High-dose ibuprofen and muscle growth', 'Young adults taking 1,200 mg of ibuprofen daily during an 8-week training program had smaller muscle gains than those taking a low dose. Occasional use for pain is fine. Daily high-dose painkillers to train through soreness is a bad idea.', 'Lilja et al., 2018'],
  ['rec', 'Antioxidant megadoses: no upside', 'High doses of vitamin C and E have blunted some training adaptations in several studies, and showed no benefit in others. There is no evidence they help muscle growth. Get antioxidants from fruit and veg, not megadose pills.'],
  ['rec', 'Stretching doesn\'t prevent soreness', 'A Cochrane review found stretching before or after exercise produced little or no reduction in muscle soreness. Stretch if you want more flexibility. Don\'t expect it to fix DOMS.', 'Herbert et al., 2011'],
  ['rec', 'Long static stretches can sap strength', 'Holding static stretches for more than about 60 seconds per muscle right before lifting can slightly reduce strength. Short stretches have little effect. Use dynamic warm-ups and ramp-up sets instead.', 'Behm et al., 2016'],
  ['rec', 'Foam rolling: small effects', 'Foam rolling gives a short-term boost in range of motion and a small reduction in perceived soreness. It does not meaningfully improve performance or speed up recovery. Do it if it feels good; skip it if short on time.', 'Wiewelhove et al., 2019'],
  ['rec', 'Life stress slows recovery', 'People with high life stress recovered strength more slowly after a hard session than people with low stress. Your nervous system does not separate gym stress from work or exam stress. In stressful weeks, keep intensity but consider fewer sets.', 'Stults-Kolehmainen et al., 2014'],
  ['rec', 'Overtraining is rare, under-recovering is common', 'True overtraining syndrome is rare and mostly seen in endurance athletes. Most lifters who feel "overtrained" are under-slept and under-fed. Fix food and sleep before blaming training volume.'],
  ['rec', 'Training sore is usually fine', 'Mild soreness does not mean a muscle can\'t be trained productively. A warm-up usually reduces it, and performance is often normal. Severe soreness that drops performance is a sign to ease off, not mild aches.'],
  ['rec', 'Tendons adapt slower than muscle', 'Muscle strength can rise quickly, but tendons and ligaments adapt more slowly. Sudden big jumps in load or volume can outpace connective tissue. Progress gradually, especially on new exercises.'],
  ['rec', 'Pain is information', 'Dull muscle fatigue is normal. Sharp, joint-specific or worsening pain is a signal to change something: range, load or exercise choice. Training through sharp pain usually turns a small issue into a long one.'],
  ['rec', 'Naps help', 'A 20–30 minute nap can improve alertness and performance, especially after a short night. It does not fully replace lost night sleep. Avoid napping late in the afternoon if it makes your night worse.'],
  ['rec', 'Consistent sleep timing matters', 'Going to bed and waking at similar times every day, including weekends, stabilises your body clock. Irregular sleep is linked with worse sleep quality. Big weekend lie-ins are a kind of jet lag.'],
  ['rec', 'Cool, dark rooms help sleep', 'A bedroom around 16–19°C helps most people sleep better. Darkness supports melatonin release. Blackout blinds and a cool room are cheap sleep upgrades.'],
  ['rec', 'Bright light in the morning, dim at night', 'Morning daylight helps set your body clock, making it easier to fall asleep at night. Bright screens late at night can delay sleep a bit. Get outside early, especially in Irish winters.'],
  ['rec', 'Muscle recovers in 48–72 hours', 'After a hard session, a muscle typically recovers within about 2–3 days, depending on volume and how close to failure you went. That is why training each muscle twice a week works well. Your split gives each muscle about 72 hours between sessions.'],
  ['rec', 'Warm-ups reduce injury and improve performance', 'A review found warming up improved performance in about 79% of the outcomes studied. A short general warm-up plus specific ramp-up sets is enough. Skipping warm-ups saves minutes and costs sessions.', 'Fradkin et al., 2010'],
  ['rec', 'Lifting is relatively safe', 'Injury rates in recreational weight training are roughly 1–4 per 1,000 hours, lower than in many team sports. Most injuries come from poor technique, ego lifting or sudden load jumps. Controlled, progressive training keeps the risk low.', 'Keogh & Winwood, 2017'],
  ['rec', 'The "neck check" for illness', 'A common sports medicine rule: if symptoms are above the neck (runny nose, mild sore throat), light training is usually fine. If they are below the neck (chest congestion, fever, body aches), rest. Training through a fever can be dangerous.'],
  ['rec', 'Cramps are mostly neuromuscular', 'Exercise-associated muscle cramps are now thought to be driven mainly by muscle fatigue and altered nerve signalling, not just dehydration or electrolytes. Stretching the cramping muscle usually stops it. Better conditioning reduces how often they happen.'],
  ['rec', 'Elbow pain from arm training', 'Achy elbows are common with heavy curls, skull crushers and pushdowns. Changing grip (EZ bar, rope, neutral), controlling the lowering phase and avoiding too much heavy volume usually helps. Pushing through joint pain makes it worse.'],
  ['rec', 'Active recovery is optional', 'Light movement on rest days, like walking, can help you feel less stiff. It does not dramatically speed recovery. It does keep your daily energy expenditure up, which helps in a cut.'],
  ['rec', 'Massage reduces soreness slightly', 'Massage after hard exercise can modestly reduce soreness and improve perceived recovery. It does not significantly improve strength recovery. It is a nice extra, not a necessity.'],
  ['rec', 'Compression gear: small effects', 'Compression garments may slightly reduce soreness and swelling after hard exercise. Effects on performance are small or absent. Wear them if you like them.'],
  ['rec', 'Sauna is mostly relaxation', 'Sauna use is linked with cardiovascular benefits in observational studies and can feel relaxing. It does not burn meaningful fat; weight lost is water. Rehydrate afterwards.'],
  ['rec', 'Sleep need is individual', 'Most adults need 7–9 hours of sleep, and young adults often need towards the upper end. If you rely on an alarm and sleep in on weekends, you are probably under-sleeping. Aim for consistent 8 hours in bed.'],
  ['rec', 'Melatonin is a timing signal, not a sedative', 'Melatonin helps shift your body clock, for example after travel. It is not a strong sleep drug. In Ireland it is prescription-only; better sleep habits come first.'],
  ['rec', 'Training time of day', 'Strength and power often peak in the late afternoon, but long-term gains are similar whether you train in the morning or evening. Consistency matters more than timing. Train when you can do it reliably.'],
  ['rec', 'Recovery basics beat recovery gadgets', 'Sleep, food, protein and sensible training volume account for most recovery. Gadgets, supplements and therapies add little on top. Fix the basics before spending money.'],
  ['rec', 'A deficit slows recovery', 'In a calorie deficit, recovery between sessions is slower and soreness can last longer. That is normal. Keep volume steady, prioritise sleep, and don\'t add extra sessions in a cut.'],
  ['rec', 'Heart rate variability as a recovery signal', 'Heart rate variability tends to drop with poor sleep, stress and illness. Some athletes use it to guide training. Day-to-day readings are noisy, so look at weekly trends.'],
  ['rec', 'Recovery is sleep, food, and time', 'No supplement or technique comes close to the effect of sleeping enough and eating enough. If you sleep 6 hours and eat 1,800 kcal, you will not recover well regardless of anything else. Fix the basics.'],
  ['rec', 'Grip and hand care', 'Torn calluses can stop you training for days. Use chalk, keep calluses filed down and don\'t let them build into ridges. Straps and grips help on heavy pulling.'],
  ['rec', 'Lower back fatigue is normal after deadlifts', 'A tight, tired lower back after deadlifts or rack pulls is common. Sharp or radiating pain is not. Reduce load and check technique if you feel anything sharp.'],
  ['rec', 'Shoulder health without overhead work', 'Face pulls, rear delt work and good rowing volume support shoulder health by balancing the pressing muscles. Your program has plenty of these. Keep them in even when time is short.'],
  ['rec', 'Hot weather and performance', 'Heat increases fatigue and dehydration risk, and strength endurance can drop. Drink more and expect slightly lower numbers on hot days. Ireland rarely has this problem.'],
  ['rec', 'Recovery after a missed night', 'One bad night has a small effect on strength but a bigger effect on mood and motivation. Train anyway, expect slightly lower numbers, and get to bed early. Two or three bad nights in a row are a bigger problem.'],
  ['rec', 'The repeated bout effect', 'The first time you do a new exercise, soreness can be severe; after a few sessions, the same workload causes much less. Your muscles adapt to protect against damage. New exercises should start with fewer sets.'],
  ['rec', 'Recovery is not linear', 'Some weeks you will feel strong and others flat for no obvious reason. Fluctuations of a few percent in strength are normal. Look at multi-week trends, not single sessions.'],
  ['rec', 'Meal timing and sleep', 'A very large meal right before bed can disrupt sleep for some people, while going to bed hungry can too. A moderate protein-rich snack 1–2 hours before bed works for most. Experiment and notice.'],
  ['rec', 'Fatigue is not always physical', 'Mental fatigue can reduce training performance even when muscles are fresh. Hard workdays can make sessions feel harder. Caffeine and a short warm-up can help, but sometimes just showing up and doing the work is enough.'],
  ['rec', 'Light days still count', 'If you feel genuinely beat up, doing the session at the same weights with fewer sets is better than skipping. You keep the habit and most of the stimulus. Missing sessions breaks momentum far more than a light day.'],
  ['rec', 'Screens before bed', 'The effect of screen light on sleep is real but smaller than often claimed. The bigger issue is that engaging content keeps you awake later. Set a phone cut-off time.'],
  ['rec', 'Injury prevention comes from load management', 'Most training injuries come from doing too much too soon. Gradual increases in load and volume, and respecting pain, prevent most issues. Strength training itself reduces sports injury risk.'],

  // ---------------- Cardio ----------------
  ['car', 'The interference effect is smaller than feared', 'Early meta-analyses found cardio reduced strength and size gains, especially with running and high frequency. A 2022 meta-analysis found concurrent training did not reduce hypertrophy or maximal strength, though explosive strength was slightly blunted. Moderate cardio is compatible with your goals.', 'Wilson et al., 2012; Schumann et al., 2022'],
  ['car', 'Cycling interferes less than running', 'Running involves heavy eccentric loading on the legs, which adds muscle damage and fatigue. Cycling is mostly concentric and interferes less with leg training. That is why your post-session cardio is on the bike.', 'Wilson et al., 2012'],
  ['car', 'Easy means conversational', 'Easy runs should be at a pace where you can speak in full sentences. Most people run their easy days too hard. Keep them genuinely easy so they don\'t eat into leg recovery.'],
  ['car', 'Sprint intervals are time-efficient', 'Six sessions of 4–6 × 30-second all-out sprints over two weeks doubled endurance time to exhaustion in a 2005 study. Later work showed similar muscle adaptations to traditional endurance training. Sprint work gives a lot of fitness for little time.', 'Burgomaster et al., 2005; Gibala et al., 2006'],
  ['car', 'Fitness predicts lifespan', 'A large 2018 study of over 120,000 patients found higher cardiorespiratory fitness was linked to lower mortality, with no upper limit to the benefit. Being in the lowest fitness group carried more risk than smoking. Your runs are a long-term investment.', 'Mandsager et al., 2018'],
  ['car', 'Running costs about 1 kcal per kg per km', 'Running burns roughly 1 kcal per kg of bodyweight per kilometre, fairly independent of pace. At 74 kg, a 5 km run burns about 370 kcal. Cardio helps a deficit, but food controls it.'],
  ['car', 'Fasted cardio doesn\'t burn more fat', 'A 2014 study found no difference in fat loss between fasted and fed cardio when calories were matched. You may burn more fat during the session, but your body compensates over the day. Do cardio when it suits you.', 'Schoenfeld et al., 2014'],
  ['car', 'The fat-burning zone is a myth', 'Low-intensity cardio uses a higher percentage of fat as fuel, but higher intensity burns more total calories. Fat loss depends on overall energy balance, not the fuel used during exercise. Pick intensity based on recovery and enjoyment.'],
  ['car', 'Afterburn is small', 'Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption (EPOC) adds only a modest number of calories after a session, typically around 6–15% of the session\'s energy cost. It is not a major fat loss factor. Count the session, not the afterburn.'],
  ['car', 'HIIT and steady cardio give similar fat loss', 'A 2017 meta-analysis found HIIT and moderate steady cardio produced similar fat loss. HIIT saves time but is harder to recover from. Use both: steady for volume, sprints for fitness.', 'Wewege et al., 2017'],
  ['car', 'People eat back exercise calories', 'Exercise increases appetite, and people often eat back a large share of the calories burned. Total energy expenditure also rises less than expected with more activity. Log your food honestly and don\'t "earn" extra calories with cardio.', 'Pontzer et al., 2016'],
  ['car', 'Daily movement beats gym cardio', 'Non-exercise activity, like walking and standing, often burns more calories than gym cardio. Desk jobs reduce it drastically. Hitting a daily step count is one of the best tools for a cut.'],
  ['car', 'The 10,000 step origin', 'The 10,000 steps target came from a 1960s Japanese pedometer marketing campaign, not science. Research finds mortality benefits rise up to roughly 8,000–10,000 steps a day for adults under 60, then plateau. More steps still burn more calories.', 'Paluch et al., 2022'],
  ['car', 'Walk after meals', 'Light walking after meals, even for a few minutes, lowers the post-meal blood sugar rise. It is easy to fit into a desk job. A 10-minute walk after lunch and dinner adds steps and helps digestion.', 'Buffey et al., 2022'],
  ['car', 'Cardio after lifting, not before', 'Cardio before lifting can reduce strength and the number of reps you can do. Doing it after protects lifting performance. That is why your bike session comes at the end.'],
  ['car', 'Separate hard cardio from leg days', 'Hard sprint sessions fatigue the legs. Keeping them away from heavy leg days, or with at least 6 hours between them, protects both. Put sprints on an upper-body day or a separate time.'],
  ['car', 'Hamstrings and sprinting', 'Sprinting puts the hamstrings under very high load, making them the most common sprint injury. A thorough warm-up and gradual build-up reduce risk. Nordic hamstring curls have been shown to cut hamstring injuries by around half in team sports.', 'van der Horst et al., 2015'],
  ['car', 'Hill sprints are safer', 'Sprinting uphill reduces top speed and the stretch load on the hamstrings while still giving a hard effort. They are a good option if you\'re new to sprinting or worried about injury. Walk back down for recovery.'],
  ['car', 'Sprint quality over fatigue', 'Sprint work should be fast and fully recovered: 6–10 efforts of 10–30 seconds with 1–3 minutes rest. When speed drops noticeably, stop. Sprints done tired are just hard cardio.'],
  ['car', 'Max heart rate formulas are rough', 'The 220 minus age formula can be off by 10–12 beats per minute for individuals. A more accurate formula is 208 minus 0.7 times age, which gives about 193 for you. Perceived effort and the talk test are often good enough.', 'Tanaka et al., 2001'],
  ['car', 'Aerobic fitness helps lifting recovery', 'Better aerobic fitness can improve recovery between hard sets by speeding up phosphocreatine resynthesis. That may help you maintain performance across multiple sets. Easy cardio supports your lifting, within limits.'],
  ['car', 'The 80/20 approach', 'Elite endurance athletes do roughly 80% of training at low intensity and 20% hard. Doing most sessions easy builds fitness without heavy fatigue. Your two easy runs and one sprint session follow the same idea.'],
  ['car', 'Lactic acid is not the enemy', 'Lactate is a fuel the body recycles, cleared within about an hour after exercise. It does not cause next-day soreness. The burn during hard sets comes from a combination of metabolites, not lactic acid alone.'],
  ['car', 'Fitness response is partly genetic', 'In the HERITAGE study, people doing identical cardio training saw very different improvements in VO2max, with heritability of the response estimated around 47%. Some people respond slowly. Consistency still improves everyone.', 'Bouchard et al., 1999'],
  ['car', 'Treadmill and outdoor running', 'Setting a treadmill to 1% incline roughly matches the energy cost of running outdoors at moderate speeds. Use it on wet Dublin days. Pace on a treadmill can feel different, so go by effort.', 'Jones & Doust, 1996'],
  ['car', 'The 10% rule has weak support', 'The common advice to increase running mileage by no more than 10% a week did not reduce injuries in a study of novice runners. Gradual progress still makes sense. Watch for pain rather than following a strict rule.', 'Buist et al., 2008'],
  ['car', 'Running shoes: comfort is the best guide', 'Research does not show that specific shoe types prevent injuries reliably. Choosing a shoe that feels comfortable is a reasonable approach. Replace shoes when they feel dead.', 'Nigg et al., 2015'],
  ['car', 'Resting heart rate drops with fitness', 'As cardiovascular fitness improves, resting heart rate usually falls. It is a simple long-term fitness marker. A sudden rise can signal illness, poor sleep or overreaching.'],
  ['car', 'Exercise suppresses appetite briefly', 'Hard exercise often reduces hunger for a short time afterwards. The effect is temporary, and appetite usually catches up later. Plan a protein-rich meal after training anyway.'],
  ['car', 'Cardio and mood', 'A 2023 umbrella review found physical activity was effective at reducing symptoms of depression and anxiety, comparable to or better than standard treatments in some cases. Cardio is good for your head as well as your heart. Easy runs are a mental reset.', 'Singh et al., 2023'],
  ['car', 'The minimum cardio for health', 'Public health guidelines recommend at least 150 minutes of moderate or 75 minutes of vigorous activity per week. Your runs, sprints and bike sessions meet this easily. More is fine, as long as it doesn\'t hurt your lifting.'],
  ['car', 'Bike setup matters', 'A saddle too low strains the knees; too high makes you rock. With the pedal at the bottom, your knee should be slightly bent. Spend a minute adjusting it before your post-session ride.'],
  ['car', 'Rowing is full-body cardio', 'Rowing machines work legs, back and arms with low impact. They can be a good alternative to running. Technique matters: legs, then hips, then arms.'],
  ['car', 'Steady cardio builds an aerobic base', 'Low-intensity cardio increases capillary density and mitochondria in muscle, improving endurance and recovery. These adaptations take weeks to months. Consistency matters more than intensity.'],
  ['car', 'Interval rest matters', 'For sprint intervals, longer rest keeps quality high and trains speed and power. For conditioning intervals, shorter rest trains endurance. Choose rest based on the goal of the session.'],
  ['car', 'Cardio for leg-focused lifters', 'Lots of running can interfere with leg recovery, while cycling interferes less. Keep running easy and limited, as in your plan. Legs are your priority; cardio supports them, not the other way around.'],
  ['car', 'Athlete\'s heart is normal', 'Regular endurance training enlarges the heart chambers and can lower resting heart rate. This is a healthy adaptation, not a disease. It reverses partially with less training.'],
  ['car', 'Walking burns fewer calories than running per km', 'Walking burns roughly half to two-thirds of the calories per kilometre that running does. But it is easy to recover from and can be done daily. A daily walk adds up over a cut.'],
  ['car', 'Cardio adherence matters most', 'The best cardio is the one you will do consistently. Running, cycling, rowing and incline walking all work. Pick what fits your schedule and doesn\'t wreck your legs.'],
  ['car', 'Cold weather running', 'Running in cold weather is safe with proper layering. Your body warms quickly, so dress as if it\'s 10°C warmer than it is. Warm up a little longer to reduce hamstring and calf strain risk.'],
  ['car', 'Sprints build power, not just fitness', 'Sprinting recruits fast-twitch muscle fibres and trains power. It can help maintain athleticism during a cut. Treat sprints as a skill session, not just conditioning.'],
  ['car', 'Too much cardio can hurt lifting', 'Very high cardio volume, especially running, can reduce recovery and strength gains. Moderate amounts are fine. If your leg numbers drop, check your cardio volume first.'],
  ['car', 'Cardio burns fewer calories than you think', 'A 30-minute easy bike ride might burn 200–300 kcal, about the same as a snack bar and a coffee. Cardio helps, but it\'s no substitute for controlling food intake. Treat it as a bonus, not a licence to eat more.'],
  ['car', 'Breathing during cardio', 'Breathing hard during sprints is normal; nasal-only breathing has no proven performance benefit. For easy runs, comfortable breathing is the guide. Don\'t overthink it.'],
  ['car', 'Cardio and fat oxidation', 'Regular aerobic training improves your muscles\' ability to use fat for fuel. This helps endurance but doesn\'t mean faster fat loss. Fat loss still depends on energy balance.'],
  ['car', 'Bike after lifting as recovery', 'Easy cycling after lifting increases blood flow and may help you cool down. It adds a small amount of calorie burn. Keep it easy so it doesn\'t add fatigue.'],
  ['car', 'VO2max improves quickly', 'Untrained people can improve VO2max by 10–20% in a few months of regular training. Trained people improve more slowly. Even small improvements are linked with better health outcomes.'],
  ['car', 'Stitches are poorly understood', 'The side stitch during running has no definitive cause, though theories involve irritation of the abdominal lining. Slowing down, deep breathing and avoiding large meals before running help. It usually passes quickly.'],
  ['car', 'Sprinting and leg muscle', 'Sprinting can maintain or slightly increase leg muscle, particularly the hamstrings and glutes. It is a useful addition for a lagging lower body. Keep volume moderate.'],
  ['car', 'Steps in a cut', 'As you diet, you unconsciously move less. Tracking steps helps prevent this drop. Keeping steps steady (e.g., 8,000–10,000) protects your deficit.'],
];
